---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import symptomsImage from '../../assets/images/symptoms-body.webp';
---
<BaseLayout title="Food Intolerance Symptoms - 8 Common Signs - FoodCheck" description="Explore common food intolerance symptoms — bloating, diarrhea, gas, headaches, fatigue, skin issues, nausea, and joint pain. See which foods trigger each one." breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Symptoms', href: '/symptoms' }]} medical={true}>
<Header /><main class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 md:py-24">
<h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-4 tracking-tight">Food Intolerance Symptoms</h1>
<p class="text-lg text-slate-500 mb-4 max-w-2xl">Food intolerances can cause a wide range of symptoms. Here are the most common ones to watch for.</p>
<p class="text-sm text-slate-400 mb-12 max-w-2xl">Reviewed by Dr. Emily Carter, Registered Dietitian</p>
<Image src={symptomsImage} alt="Human body illustration showing common digestive and physical symptoms" class="w-full h-auto rounded-xl mb-10 max-w-2xl" width={800} height={450} loading="lazy" format="webp" />
<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-5">What Are the Most Common Food Intolerance Symptoms?</h2>
<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-5">
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Bloating</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-2">Abdominal distension and discomfort after eating. Common with lactose, fructose, and FODMAP intolerances.</p><p class="text-xs text-slate-400"><strong>Common triggers:</strong> Dairy products, wheat, onions, garlic, beans, carbonated drinks. According to the BDA, bloating is the most frequently reported food intolerance symptom.</p></div>
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Diarrhoea</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-2">Loose or watery stools, often within hours of eating trigger foods. A hallmark of many food intolerances.</p><p class="text-xs text-slate-400"><strong>Common triggers:</strong> Lactose, fructose, sugar alcohols (sorbitol, mannitol), fatty foods. Persistent diarrhoea should always be evaluated by your GP to rule out other conditions.</p></div>
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Gas / Flatulence</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-2">Excessive wind caused by undigested food fermenting in the large intestine.</p><p class="text-xs text-slate-400"><strong>Common triggers:</strong> Beans, lentils, cabbage, broccoli, carbonated drinks, high-fibre foods. Gas combined with bloating often suggests FODMAP sensitivity.</p></div>
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Headache / Migraine</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-2">Recurring headaches after meals, especially associated with histamine and tyramine-rich foods.</p><p class="text-xs text-slate-400"><strong>Common triggers:</strong> Aged cheese, red wine, cured meats, chocolate, citrus fruits. Histamine intolerance is a common but underrecognised cause of diet-related headaches.</p></div>
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Fatigue & Brain Fog</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-2">Unusual tiredness, difficulty concentrating, and mental sluggishness following meals.</p><p class="text-xs text-slate-400"><strong>Common triggers:</strong> Gluten (in non-coeliac gluten sensitivity), high-sugar meals, large portions. Fatigue that persists despite adequate sleep warrants investigation by your GP.</p></div>
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Skin Issues</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-2">Eczema, hives, flushing, or itchy skin that may be linked to histamine or other food triggers.</p><p class="text-xs text-slate-400"><strong>Common triggers:</strong> Histamine-rich foods (aged cheese, wine, fermented foods), food additives, sulphites. Skin symptoms can also indicate a true food allergy — consult your GP if reactions are severe.</p></div>
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Nausea</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-2">Feeling sick or queasy after eating certain foods. Can occur with multiple intolerance types.</p><p class="text-xs text-slate-400"><strong>Common triggers:</strong> Fatty or greasy foods, dairy, spicy foods, alcohol. Nausea that occurs consistently after a specific food is a strong indicator of intolerance.</p></div>
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Joint Pain</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-2">Aching joints that correlate with food intake, reported in gluten sensitivity and other intolerances.</p><p class="text-xs text-slate-400"><strong>Common triggers:</strong> Gluten (in sensitivity or coeliac disease), nightshade vegetables (for some individuals), processed foods. Joint pain with digestive symptoms may suggest a systemic food reaction.</p></div>
</div>
<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-5">Which Intolerance Types Should You Check?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-6">If you recognise any of the symptoms above, use our free checkers to explore further:</p>
<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4">
<a href="/tools/lactose-intolerance-test" class="block bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-brand-300 hover:shadow-sm transition-all no-underline"><span class="text-2xl mb-2 block">🥛</span><p class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 mb-1">Lactose Checker</p><p class="text-xs text-slate-500">Dairy-related symptoms assessment</p></a>
<a href="/tools/gluten-intolerance-test" class="block bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-brand-300 hover:shadow-sm transition-all no-underline"><span class="text-2xl mb-2 block">🌾</span><p class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 mb-1">Gluten Checker</p><p class="text-xs text-slate-500">Wheat and gluten sensitivity</p></a>
<a href="/tools/histamine-intolerance-test" class="block bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-brand-300 hover:shadow-sm transition-all no-underline"><span class="text-2xl mb-2 block">🍷</span><p class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 mb-1">Histamine Checker</p><p class="text-xs text-slate-500">Reactions to aged and fermented foods</p></a>
<a href="/tools/fructose-intolerance-test" class="block bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-5 hover:border-brand-300 hover:shadow-sm transition-all no-underline"><span class="text-2xl mb-2 block">🍎</span><p class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 mb-1">Fructose Checker</p><p class="text-xs text-slate-500">Fruit and sweetener malabsorption</p></a>
</div>
<div class="mt-6"><a href="/tools/food-diary" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-5 py-2.5 rounded-xl bg-brand-600 text-white text-sm font-medium hover:bg-brand-700 transition-colors no-underline shadow-sm">Start a Food Diary<svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"/><polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"/></svg></a></div>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-5">How Can You Tell If Symptoms Are From Food Intolerance?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">Not all digestive symptoms are caused by food intolerances. According to the <strong>NHS</strong>, food intolerance symptoms typically share these characteristics:</p>
<div class="space-y-3 mb-8">
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1 text-sm">Timing</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Symptoms usually appear within a few hours of eating the trigger food, but can be delayed up to 48 hours. This differs from food allergies, where reactions typically occur within minutes to 2 hours.</p></div>
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1 text-sm">Dose-dependence</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">The severity of symptoms often depends on the amount of food consumed. A small amount of lactose may cause no symptoms, while a full glass of milk triggers bloating and diarrhoea. This is a key difference from food allergies, where even trace amounts can cause severe reactions.</p></div>
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1 text-sm">Consistency</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Symptoms recur each time the trigger food is consumed. If your bloating happens every time you eat pasta but not when you eat rice, gluten may be the culprit. Keeping a <a href="/tools/food-diary" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 underline">food and symptom diary</a> for 2–4 weeks is the most effective way to identify these patterns.</p></div>
<div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4"><h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1 text-sm">Non-life-threatening</h3><p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Food intolerance symptoms, while uncomfortable, are not life-threatening. If you experience difficulty breathing, swelling of the face or throat, or loss of consciousness after eating, this may be a food allergy and requires immediate emergency medical attention.</p></div>
</div>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-5">When Should You See a Doctor?</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">According to the NHS, you should see your GP if you have persistent digestive symptoms that do not improve with dietary changes, unexplained weight loss, blood in stools, or difficulty swallowing. These may indicate conditions beyond food intolerance, such as coeliac disease or inflammatory bowel disease (IBD).</p>
<blockquote class="border-l-4 border-brand-400 bg-white rounded-r-xl p-5 shadow-sm mb-6">
  <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 italic mb-2">"Food intolerance symptoms can be similar to those of more serious conditions. Always see your GP if symptoms are severe, persistent, or affecting your quality of life."</p>
  <cite class="text-xs text-slate-500 not-italic">— NHS UK, Food Intolerance (2024)</cite>
</blockquote>

